Date: Mon, 19 Apr 93 18:05:31 EDT
From: eisrael@suneast.East.Sun.COM (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: Korzybski's "E-Prime", also NLP

Derek Zahn writes:
> They give some "word problems" about addition to exemplify the
> problems -- many people know how to add  but do not know when
> to add.

Well, that's the stuff of sapience, isn't it? After all, it can be said
that a computer knows how to add but not when. It can add better and
faster than a human, but give it a word problem at it will stare at you
with that mechanical look that says "You're the one with higher brain
functions, you tell me."

But I'm not clear on how this relates to the question of using formal
systems to model physical systems. And, if the "mathematics by fiat"
view (which seems quite reasonable to me) explains the preponderance of
formal systems used for modeling, we're still left with the question of
"why." What is it about the expressiveness of these systems that let's
you bridge them over to areas where their rules don't apply? Is it just
the ability to encode increasing amounts of uncertainty or is there
some other reason? Is it the augmentation of formal systems by other
cognitive tools that makes this bridge possible? (For example, how did
Kepler think to prefer his model of a Sol-centered cosmos with
elliptical planetary orbits when the system of cycles and epicycles in
an Earth-centered cosmos also seemed to work?)

Silly questions perhaps, but it seems to me that the "mathematics by
fiat" view argues that formal systems aren't the best tool for at least
some kinds of mental exploration. If I'd been using a butterknife for
years to drive screws and then found that, although it worked just
fine, a butterknife isn't really the right tool for the job, I'd want
to know what it was about the butterknife that worked so that I'd
recognize the right tool when I saw it.

Elias Israel
