Date: Mon, 19 Apr 93 18:55 EDT
From: kqb@whscad1.att.com
Subject: CRYPTO/MONEY: Make a Fortune Off Wiretap Chip?

I am afraid that the flurry of crypto-related messages on Extropians,
sci.crypt, etc. is powered mostly by adrenalin and that as soon as
the excitement wears off we will return to business-as-usual.
If anyone on this list can turn the Wiretap Chip affair into a
profit-making business, though, then I would be much more confident
of a long-term effort to provide ubiquitous secure encryption services.

To illustrate some of the kinds of opportunities the Wiretap Chip
might create, I have listed below the possibilities that popped
into my head today.  I make no claims of feasibility or even
desirability, but suggest that these may stimulate thinking toward
ideas that actually are useful.

  (1) Sell the "secret" Clipper chip algorithm.  It shouldn't take
      long to reverse engineer the chip and deduce the algorithm.
      Who has the equipment & expertise to do that and how long will
      that information have market value?  (After it loses market
      value, one might as well post it on USENET, I suppose, or
      would that prompt the authorities to clamp down on USENET?)

  (2) Can this algorithm be broken?  If so, how easy is it to break?
      For example, if I knew a good way to break RSA, I probably
      would not tell anyone that I knew how to break it.  Instead,
      I would offer a discreet "intelligence service" and let
      everyone else think that their RSA-encoded messages were
      secure.  Similarly, if you can break the Clipper chip algorithm,
      but your method is not at all obvious, you may have a wealth
      of private/sensitive information readily available that has a
      high market value.

  (3) "Stealth" chips that mimic Clipper chips but are actually
      cryptographically secure.  How hard would it be to "hack"
      the Clipper chip masks, once they have been reverse engineered?
      Or would it be as simple as just creating chips with the same
      design, but not keeping a record of the chip's secret keys?
      In that case, a simple clone chip could be a "stealth" chip.
      (To ensure the customer that *you* haven't recorded the secret
      keys, could one make the chips user-programmable?)

  (4) Sell to the government a technology that can remotely identify
      any "stealth" chips and then go make a second generation of
      "stealth" chips that cannot be identified (until your third
      generation of "stealth" chips is ready).

  (5) It's probably too late to act now, but did the stock price for
      that Clipper chip military contracter jump up when the govt.
      made the Clipper chip announcement?

  (6) Illicit opportunities.  In the book "Cat's Cradle", the
      religion of "Bokononism" was ensured its popularity by
      making it illegal.  Perhaps the govt.'s War On Privacy
      will make encryption as popular and lucrative as the govt.'s
      War On Drugs has made the drug business.  (Unfortunately,
      it might also make the business as deadly, making it
      unpopular for immortalists who can't distance themselves
      from the action.)  A simple PGP-on-a-chip product may be a
      big money-maker on the black market.

  (7) Propaganda opportunities.  Don't just flame and complain.
      Entertain!  If you can write books and movies, make a
      profit while educating the public about the consequences
      of the War On Privacy.

  (8) Where can I find the state-of-the-art in steganography?
      (Oh yes, it's right in front of me.  I just didn't notice it!)
      An automated process for hiding messages in poetry sounds quite
      difficult to create, unless one has a taste for lousy poetry.
      Hiding messages in grainy b/w photos sounds a lot easier
      (unless one uses lossy compression on them, of course).
      alt.steganography.pictures anyone?

Disclaimer.  If I was able and willing to do any of the above, I
wouldn't be telling everyone on Extropians about them, so take these
suggestions for what they are worth.  Meanwhile, I'll be working
on the idea I *didn't* tell you about... :-)
