alternatives to accepting a number of ideological restrictions on their
lives. Under these conditions, couldn't some restriction upon the possible
employment agreements increase freedom? 

>
>>7) The difference between employment contracts and murder contracts lies
>>entirely in our attitudes toward them.
>
>This is too vague[. . .].
>T.O. Morrow       

What I mean is that the dominant memes in our society make murder contracts
uncontroversially unenforceable by public courts, while there is no
concensus on employment contracts. Anyone who tried to sue in court for
damages or specific performance of a murder contract would have to be
loony. Any judge that agreed to hear the case, much less award damages,
would probably face impeachment. If such judgement was made and was not
overturned by higher courts, a revolution of some kind would occur. Our
attidude (the attitude of persons in North America) on murder contracts is
quite clear. But there is nothing inevitable about that attitude, and so.
While it is unlikely that some society would ever reach as complete a
consensus on employment as on murder, the principle is the same. Beliefs,
memes, are the bedrock underneath the system. These are influenced but not
determined by practical questions. (e.g. arbitrary murder is so impractical
that an example of a society that left it legal could be found. But the
borders between what is murder and what is not shift between societies and
historical periods.) Schelling points are cultural phenomena, and multiple
equilibria are possible.

 
