In <9304192213.AA12349@churchy.gnu.ai.mit.edu>, Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering writes:
|> that a computer knows how to add but not when. It can add better and
|> faster than a human, but give it a word problem at it will stare at you
|> with that mechanical look that says "You're the one with higher brain
|> functions, you tell me."
Cute -- but I never could tell that from the mechanical look that
says "You have the parentheses in the wrong place" ;-)

|> But I'm not clear on how this relates to the question of using formal
|> systems to model physical systems. And, if the "mathematics by fiat"
|> view (which seems quite reasonable to me) explains the preponderance of
|> formal systems used for modeling, we're still left with the question of
|> "why."

Here's my take on the "unreasonable applicability" phenom, stripped of
philosophy-of-math jargon. Mathematics is generally cumulative, that
is, building on experience (at least since Euclid). Now, mathematical
problems were originally chosen for their applicability, and only
later were the gaps filled in with formal theories. We have done
mathematics for 4000 years, only during the last 250 of which has
there been the slightest hint of pure mathematics -- that is, math
theories so abstract that we think they can't possibly describe
anything in the real world. But essentially all of our math, and
especially our sense of what is a reasonable problem to work on, is
based on the 4000 years of flood-plain measurement, pyramid-building,
astronomy, and physics.

I had to tutor a chemistry-major friend because he suddenly found
himself in an advanced course in organic chemistry where he needed
group theory, because that was the right formalism for studying
symmetries in molecules.

Group theory was developed as an aid to solving polynomial equations,
and as a way of understanding permutation. If a formal concept is
broad enough, other things fall into it, the way that certain aspects
of geometry eventually fed back into group theory.

Analysts may work for years on an obscure kind of system of PDEs, only
to find that shortly after they get a handle on them formally, they
have become important in astrophysics. Or, to be fair, often the
physicists get there first, demonstrate some results, and importantly,
demonstrate that a particular class of system is "interesting" and
math guys will mop up later.

Anyway, similar paradigms are used to step up the chain of abstraction.
The first time someone showed that the elements of a group or ring or field
could just as well be functions as numbers, the ladder was against the wall,
and people would forever progress from an arithmetic level to an algebraic
level and so on.

Projective geometry is a nice 19th-century example -- from a way of
doing optics problems to a different way of looking at geometry, and
then through Desargues' Theorem to algebras over projective planes, and
later to a theory of skew-fields over the algebras over ... to be
fair, I don't remember any physical application of that level yet, but
there's no good reason that there shouldn't be, because the same
mechanisms of abstraction were used at each step -- the tried and
proven ones.
